# Trivy ignore file
#
# Added 2026-08-01 — EXPIRES / re-review 2026-09-01.
#
# msgpack and setuptools are NOT dependencies of this application: neither
# appears in poetry.lock and nothing in the app imports them. They exist purely
# as code vendored inside pip:
#
#   /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack
#   /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/_vendor/pkg_resources
#
# Trivy 0.55.0 resolves python-pkg metadata from the image's layer history
# rather than the final squashed filesystem, and pip ships inside the
# python:3.13-slim base layer — so even deleting the whole pip toolchain in a
# later layer does not retract the finding. Verified in adamselir/audit-runner
# with a --no-cache rebuild plus an in-image `find /`: the files are genuinely
# absent from the running container, yet Trivy still reports them.
#
# Drop both entries when the pinned Trivy version is upgraded; newer releases
# may resolve layer whiteouts correctly.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964  # msgpack — vendored inside pip
CVE-2025-47273       # setuptools/pkg_resources — vendored inside pip

# Added 2026-10-02 — EXPIRES / re-review 2026-11-01.
#
# urllib3 is NOT a dependency of this application: it is absent from
# poetry.lock and nothing imports it. The flagged urllib3 2.7.0 is the copy
# vendored inside pip (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt pins urllib3==2.7.0 in pip
# 26.2.1, the latest release as of 2026-10-02), shipped in the
# python:3.13-slim base layer. Trivy 0.55 reports pip-vendored packages from
# the base layer, so no later layer (pip upgrade or deletion) retracts it.
# Drop these once a pip release vendors urllib3 >= 2.8.0 and python:3.13-slim
# is rebuilt with it.
CVE-2026-97687       # urllib3 — vendored inside pip (HTTPS proxy TLS interception)
CVE-2026-97689       # urllib3 — vendored inside pip (unbounded memory DoS)
